Yes. Your current configuration is a good candidate for converting into three Terraform modules:

Root Module
   │
   ├── Networking Module
   │      ├── VNet
   │      ├── Subnet
   │      ├── NSG
   │      └── NSG Rules
   │
   ├── Compute Module
   │      ├── NICs
   │      └── Linux VMs
   │
   └── Load Balancer Module
          ├── Public IP
          ├── Load Balancer
          ├── Backend Pool
          ├── Health Probe
          ├── LB Rule
          └── NIC → Backend Association

The important Terraform concept is that the local machine only calls the root module, while the root module calls the modules stored in GitHub.

1. Final GitHub repository structure

Create a GitHub repository such as:

terraform-azure-web-platform

Use this structure:

terraform-azure-web-platform/
│
├── modules/
│   │
│   ├── networking/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   │
│   ├── compute/
│   │   ├── main.tf
│   │   ├── variables.tf
│   │   └── outputs.tf
│   │
│   └── loadbalancer/
│       ├── main.tf
│       ├── variables.tf
│       └── outputs.tf
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── locals.tf
├── provider.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── terraform.tfvars
└── app/
    └── app.sh

I recommend not putting your Azure backend configuration inside the reusable modules. The backend belongs in the root configuration.

2. Root provider.tf
terraform {
  required_providers {
    azurerm = {
      source  = "hashicorp/azurerm"
      version = "=5.0.0"
    }
  }


  backend "azurerm" {
    resource_group_name  = "gopal-storage-state"
    storage_account_name = "gopaltfstate"
    container_name       = "tfstate"
    key                  = "sky-project.tfstate"
  }
}


provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
}

Your state is therefore stored remotely:

Azure Storage Account
        │
        ▼
